} [:a {:href "/venues/635"} "Ol-lim-pik Ju-gyeong-gi-jang, Seoul Sports Complex, Seoul"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/63573"} "38 from 22 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The new javelin was being used, as it had been since 1986, so the marks were much shorter than the 90+ metre throws of 1968-1980. The favorite was the huge Finn, " [:a {:href "/athletes/67787"} "Seppo Räty"] ", the 1987 World Champion. Also considered was the world record holder with the implement, Czechoslovakian " [:a {:href "/athletes/66875"} "Ján Železný"] ", who led the qualifying with 85.90 (281-10), and the first man to set a world record with the new jav, " [:a {:href "/athletes/70542"} "Klaus Tafelmeier"] " (FRG). The leader in round one was another Finn, " [:a {:href "/athletes/67645"} "Tapio Korjus"] ", but he had the third-longest throw ever with the new javelin. In round two, Železný and " [:a {:href "/athletes/77474"} "Viktor Yevsyukov"] " both threw 82.32 (270-1) to take over. Räty came thru in the third round with 83.26 (273-2), and as the lead shifted around, Železný regained it in round four with 83.46 (273-10). Throwing third in the final round, he improved to 84.12 (275-2), but the last thrower was Korjus, and his 84.28 (276-6¼) brought him the gold medal, Železný claiming silver."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "303"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/67645"} "Tapio Korjus"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} "81.42 (3)"] [:td {} "84.28 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [: